WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.760
A little bit about tracebacks.

00:03.760 --> 00:08.520
We talked about how to read the traceback from a Python program earlier in the day that

00:08.520 --> 00:13.520
we want to start at the bottom and go up to the top.

00:13.520 --> 00:20.920
If we have any uncaught exceptions in our program, it is going to exit the program.

00:20.920 --> 00:36.520
So let's make a new file called exceptions.py and run this and we will see that end of the

00:36.520 --> 00:43.440
program never printed out because this through an exception I'm trying to ask Python to convert

00:43.440 --> 00:48.240
the letter A into an integer and it's like, I don't know what you're talking about.

00:48.240 --> 00:53.480
So you need to be mindful that your programs don't have any uncaught exceptions or they

00:53.480 --> 00:56.480
will behave in unpredictable ways.

00:56.480 --> 01:04.360
You want to catch your exceptions with syntax called try and accept.

01:04.360 --> 01:11.200
And let's look at what that looks like.

01:11.200 --> 01:19.360
Try is a keyword with a colon and you need to indent the code that you are trying.

01:19.360 --> 01:32.720
One level and then the accept keyword and following the accept keyword, this will work

01:32.720 --> 01:37.040
without any additional arguments.

01:37.040 --> 01:44.720
I can say an exception happened.

01:44.720 --> 01:50.600
So if I run this, it will print out an exception happened and then end of the program.

01:50.600 --> 01:57.600
Generally we want to catch exceptions that are specific as possible.

01:57.600 --> 02:01.880
And for the most part, Python gives us a helpful hint.

02:01.880 --> 02:03.640
What was the exception here?

02:03.640 --> 02:05.400
Well it was a value error.

02:05.440 --> 02:10.360
I tried to pass in a string into the integer.

02:10.360 --> 02:16.000
So I can just copy this class and paste it in after the accept.

02:16.000 --> 02:23.720
And that will catch the very specific exception that was thrown in this case.

02:23.720 --> 02:35.360
If I had another exception in here like a divide by zero, now that's also a value error.

02:35.600 --> 02:37.760
It's another good exception.

02:37.760 --> 02:52.600
But say I have a dictionary and I try to get a key that is not in that dictionary that will throw a key error.

02:52.600 --> 02:56.600
Is that also a value error?

02:56.600 --> 02:59.920
Here.

02:59.920 --> 03:03.520
Oh right, I would need to change the order of these.

03:03.560 --> 03:08.160
So because this through an exception first, it's getting caught here.

03:08.160 --> 03:10.520
I want to show you something else.

03:10.520 --> 03:14.800
This will throw a key error.

03:14.800 --> 03:19.040
And so now if I run this, I will see my key error.

03:19.040 --> 03:22.720
That's because this is the code that through the exception.

03:22.720 --> 03:28.480
If I change this back to just accept with no specific exception,

03:28.480 --> 03:30.720
it's going to catch both of them.

03:30.760 --> 03:33.480
Generally, this is a bad thing.

03:33.480 --> 03:37.320
You want to be a specific about catching your exceptions as possible,

03:37.320 --> 03:42.160
so that when you need to go back and figure out and debug and troubleshoot what weren't wrong,

03:42.160 --> 03:47.840
you have a pretty clear idea about which code caught, which exception.

03:47.840 --> 03:53.240
Now, you can also have different code run for different exceptions.

03:53.240 --> 03:59.960
So I can say accept value error, say a value exception happened.

03:59.960 --> 04:04.280
And then I can say accept key error,

04:04.280 --> 04:15.120
which I got the class from right here and print out a key was not found.

04:15.120 --> 04:19.160
And now because this was first, I got my key error first,

04:19.160 --> 04:26.720
if I switch the order of these, I will get my value error first.

04:26.720 --> 04:31.440
But now I'm caught, I have both scenarios covered.

04:31.440 --> 04:35.840
And I know exactly which exception was thrown and where.

04:35.840 --> 04:38.640
I'm not going to deep dive into exceptions,

04:38.640 --> 04:43.520
but I think they're pretty important fundamental concept of Python.

04:43.520 --> 04:50.880
So I recommend that you go back through and take a look at this detailed chapter.

04:50.960 --> 05:02.400
Most importantly, you never want to catch exceptions that aren't specific.

05:02.400 --> 05:09.360
If you try to catch from the exception, the all exceptions in herit from,

05:09.360 --> 05:16.960
you could end up with a program that doesn't let you exit it with control C or other weird and subtle bugs.

05:16.960 --> 05:24.400
So make sure that you have a pretty good understanding of how exceptions work.

05:24.400 --> 05:30.880
There's also a finally clause that you can include that is an optional block

05:30.880 --> 05:36.640
that will run after the try regardless of if an exception was thrown or not.

05:36.640 --> 05:41.360
So if you need to do some cleanup, that's the right place for it.

05:41.360 --> 05:44.960
So let's chat about the exception hierarchy.

05:44.960 --> 05:50.880
Python has a lot of useful built-in exceptions that you are probably going to encounter.

05:50.880 --> 05:56.160
You can find a detailed list of those built-in exceptions in the Python documentation.

05:56.720 --> 06:02.640
An important thing to know about exceptions is they like everything that else in Python are just objects.

06:02.640 --> 06:11.520
So they follow an inheritance hierarchy and more specific classes inherit from more general ones.

06:11.600 --> 06:16.160
For example, these zero division error is a subclass of arithmetic error,

06:16.160 --> 06:20.880
which is a subclass of exception, which is a subclass of base exception.

06:21.760 --> 06:26.560
That's why the best practice is to catch more specific exceptions first.

06:28.880 --> 06:36.640
And don't catch exception and especially don't catch base exception.

06:36.640 --> 06:41.360
If you catch base exception, you're going to swallow every type of exception.

06:41.360 --> 06:47.680
Including the one that is thrown when you press Ctrl C while a Python program is running.

06:47.680 --> 06:50.720
And that is the keyboard interrupt exception.

06:50.720 --> 06:57.040
If you catch base exception, it will cause your Python program not to respond to Ctrl C,

06:57.040 --> 07:00.240
which is not something that anybody wants. So don't do it.

07:01.280 --> 07:09.200
Because exceptions are regular classes, we can also inherit from them from our own custom exceptions,

07:09.200 --> 07:14.240
which makes it very easy to make our own custom specific exceptions.

07:14.240 --> 07:16.560
This makes their programs a lot more readable.

07:18.160 --> 07:24.800
And I'm not going to cover custom exceptions here, but there are custom exceptions that you can work through in practice.

